.TH WHOAMI 1 2026-05-11 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whoami \- print the effective user name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whoami
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR whoami
utility prints the user name associated with the current effective
user ID.  It is equivalent to
.BR "id -un" .
.PP
No operands are accepted.  Any non-option argument is treated as an
error.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The user name was printed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The effective user ID has no matching entry in the user database, or
an unexpected operand was given.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B "cannot find name for user ID N"
The effective UID
.I N
returned by
.BR geteuid (2)
has no
.BR passwd (5)
entry.  Either the user database is missing the row, or
.BR getpwuid (3)
failed for another reason.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
$ whoami
segin
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR logname (1),
.BR getpwuid (3),
.BR geteuid (2)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR whoami
is specified by POSIX.1-2024 and by long-standing BSD and GNU
practice.  Substrate's implementation matches the GNU coreutils
form: the
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
long options are honoured; all other arguments are rejected.
.SH HISTORY
.BR whoami
appeared in 2BSD as a shorthand for
.BR "id -un" .
